          An anode rod is a sacrificial metal rod that corrodes in place of your steel tank, and once it is gone, the tank starts rusting from the inside. Check it at three to five years, sooner with a water softener, and replace it when the core wire is showing or the rod is badly thinned. Anode rods typically cost well under fifty dollars at retail, the swap takes an hour with a 1-1/16 inch socket and a breaker bar, and on a heater that is still sound it can add years of life.
