        <TopPick />

        <div id="what-it-does" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What an Anode Rod Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            The inside of a tank water heater is steel coated with a thin layer of glass, sometimes called glass lining or porcelain enamel. The coating is never perfect. There are always tiny gaps, especially around the welds and fittings, and wherever bare steel touches water, it can rust. Left unchecked, that corrosion eventually eats through the tank wall, and a tank leak is the end of the heater.
          </p>

          <p>
            The anode rod is the defense. It is a long rod of a more reactive metal, usually magnesium or aluminum, threaded into the top of the tank and hanging down into the water. Because it is more reactive than steel, the corrosion process attacks the anode first. This is called galvanic or cathodic protection, and it is the same principle used to protect boat hulls and buried pipelines.
          </p>

          <p>
            As long as the anode has metal left to give, it sacrifices itself and the exposed steel in the tank is protected. Once it is used up, down to the steel core wire that runs through its center, the protection stops and the tank begins to corrode. That is why anode condition is one of the best predictors of how long a tank will last, and why replacing the anode before it is gone can extend a heater's life.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most residential tanks have one anode, either a separate rod with its own hex head on top of the tank or a combination anode built into the hot water outlet nipple. Some larger or longer-warranty tanks have two. The longer tank warranties manufacturers offer are often backed in part by heavier or additional anodes, which is one reason a 12-year tank costs more than a 6-year one.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Job in Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1-1/16"</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Common socket size</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">3-5 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical first check</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">6 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Exposed wire = replace</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~1 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical DIY time</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="when-to-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. When to Check and When to Replace
          </h2>

          <p>
            A common guideline is to inspect the anode at around three to five years and then every year or two after that. That window shifts with your water. Softened water is more aggressive toward anodes, so homes with a water softener often go through them faster and should check sooner, at two to three years. Very hard water can coat the anode in scale, which can slow its consumption but also reduces its effectiveness.
          </p>

          <p>
            What you are looking for when you pull it out is how much metal remains. A healthy anode is roughly the diameter of a thumb along its length, though it may be lumpy or coated. A worn anode is thin, pitted and may have sections where the steel core wire is exposed. A common rule of thumb is that if six inches or more of the core wire is showing, or the rod has thinned to well under its original diameter for much of its length, replace it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some anodes look like they are in good shape but are covered in a thick crust of calcium. That coating can stop the anode from working even though the metal is still there. If it flakes off easily to reveal healthy metal, you may be fine; if it is packed solid, replacing the rod is a reasonable call.
          </p>

          <p>
            Checking is also a good moment to look at the heater as a whole. If you pull out an anode that is completely gone on a tank that is ten years or older, the tank has likely been unprotected for some time, and a new anode may not save it. If you are not sure how old your heater is, our guide to <a href="/how-to-tell-the-age-of-a-water-heater/">how to tell the age of a water heater</a> explains how to read the serial number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Since you need to drain a few gallons to check the anode, it makes sense to pair this job with a sediment flush. Our <a href="/how-to-drain-a-water-heater/">how to drain a water heater guide</a> covers the steps, and doing both at once means one afternoon of maintenance a year covers the two jobs that most affect tank life.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="anode-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Magnesium vs Aluminum vs Powered Anodes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Magnesium anodes are the most reactive common option, which means they provide strong protection, and they are standard in many tanks. Their downside is that they are consumed relatively quickly, especially in softened or aggressive water, and they are the type most associated with rotten-egg smells in certain water supplies, covered below.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aluminum anodes, usually an aluminum alloy that may include zinc, are less reactive and tend to last longer, which is why they are often recommended for hard water. Aluminum-zinc anodes are the usual first fix for a smelly heater because the zinc helps counter the hydrogen sulfide reaction. Some people avoid aluminum anodes over concerns about aluminum in drinking water; EPA guidance is to use cold tap water, not hot, for drinking and cooking anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Powered anodes, sometimes called impressed-current anodes, take a different approach. Instead of a metal that dissolves, they use a titanium rod and a small electrical current from a plug-in power supply to protect the tank. Because the rod is not consumed, it does not need regular replacement. Powered anodes cost several times more than a standard rod, but they eliminate the main cause of rotten-egg smells and remove the need for periodic anode checks.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Strength</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Trade-off</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Magnesium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strongest protection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wears faster; can contribute to odor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soft to moderate water, no smell</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Aluminum / zinc</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Longer life, helps with odor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Somewhat less protection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Hard water, sulfur smell</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Powered (impressed current)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not consumed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Higher cost, needs an outlet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Persistent smell, long-term owners</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Check your heater's manual before switching types. Some manufacturers specify which anodes are approved for their tanks, and using a non-approved rod, or removing the anode entirely, can affect the tank warranty. If the heater is still under warranty, the safest choice is the replacement part the manufacturer specifies or approves.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rotten-egg-smell" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Rotten-Egg Smell Connection
          </h2>

          <p>
            If your hot water smells like rotten eggs but your cold water does not, the water heater is the likely source. The smell is hydrogen sulfide gas, and in a water heater it is usually produced by sulfate-reducing bacteria. These bacteria are common in well water and can live in some municipal supplies too. They use sulfates in the water and hydrogen produced by the anode reaction to make hydrogen sulfide, and the warm, still water in a tank is a good place for them to thrive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Magnesium anodes are the most associated with the problem because they produce more of the hydrogen the bacteria use. Switching to an aluminum-zinc anode often reduces or eliminates the smell. A powered anode tends to work even better, because it does not produce the same reaction. These are the two most common long-term fixes and the reason anode replacement and odor problems are so often discussed together.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some manuals describe disinfecting the tank, for example with a chlorine bleach or hydrogen peroxide treatment, to kill the bacteria. Follow the manufacturer's instructions exactly if you go this route, because the wrong amount or method can damage the tank or create unsafe water. Raising the tank temperature to kill bacteria is sometimes suggested too, but it creates a serious scald risk and should only be done with care and with the temperature returned to normal afterward.
          </p>

          <p>
            Removing the anode entirely will usually stop the smell, but it also removes the tank's corrosion protection and can void the warranty. It is the wrong fix. If the cold water smells too, the source is the water supply rather than the heater, and an anode change will not solve it. Our guide to <a href="/why-does-my-water-smell-like-rotten-eggs/">why water smells like rotten eggs</a> walks through how to tell the difference and what to do about each.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">QUICK TEST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Hot water only? Look at the heater. Both hot and cold? Look at the supply.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Run the cold tap and the hot tap separately and smell each. A smell on the hot side only points to bacteria and the anode in the tank. A smell on both sides points to the well or water source.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Tools, Seized Rods and Low Clearance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most anode rods have a 1-1/16 inch hex head, so the key tool is a 1-1/16 inch socket, ideally a six-point impact socket that grips the head well. Pair it with a long breaker bar, because anodes are often installed tight and can seize after years in the tank. You will also want thread sealant tape or pipe dope rated for potable water, a garden hose to drain some water, and a flashlight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Seized anodes are the most common obstacle. The hex head may be under the jacket or a plastic cap, so you may need to pry a cap off or cut some insulation away to reach it. Leverage helps more than force: a long breaker bar gives you control, and a helper holding the tank or a strap around it prevents the whole heater from twisting on its connections. Some people use an electric impact wrench, which can break a stuck rod loose with less twisting of the tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the head starts rounding off, stop and reassess. A rounded head makes the job much harder and may require extraction tools. Some owners find this is the point where a plumber visit is worth the cost, especially if the heater is in a tight space where it is difficult to get leverage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Low ceiling clearance</h3>

          <p>
            A standard anode rod is roughly the height of the tank, so pulling one straight out often requires several feet of clearance above the heater. In basements, closets and under stairs, there often is not enough room. The solution is a flexible or segmented anode, which is made of shorter linked sections that bend as you feed it in. You may need to bend the old rod as you pull it out, or cut it, to get it clear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flexible rods are sold in the same materials as rigid ones, so you can still choose magnesium or aluminum-zinc. They usually cost slightly more than a rigid rod, but they are often the only practical option where the heater sits close to the ceiling or under shelving.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="step-by-step" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Step-by-Step Anode Replacement
          </h2>

          <p>
            First, turn off the heat. On an electric heater, switch off the breaker. On a gas heater, set the control to off or vacation. Then close the cold water supply valve and open a hot faucet somewhere in the house to relieve the pressure in the tank. Let the water cool if you can, because hot water is a scald risk while you work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, drain a few gallons from the tank through the drain valve using a garden hose. You do not need to empty it completely. You only need to lower the water level below the top so water does not flow out when you remove the anode. Our <a href="/how-to-drain-a-water-heater/">draining guide</a> covers the hose setup and why the elements on an electric heater must stay off until the tank is full again.
          </p>

          <p>
            Find the anode. It is usually a hex head on top of the tank, sometimes under a cap or insulation. If the heater has a combination anode built into the hot outlet, you will need to disconnect the hot water line to reach it. Fit the 1-1/16 inch socket and breaker bar, brace the tank, and turn counterclockwise. Once it breaks loose, unscrew it and lift the rod out, bending or cutting it if clearance is tight.
          </p>

          <p>
            Inspect the old rod, then wrap the threads of the new one with sealant tape or apply pipe dope. Feed the new rod into the opening, thread it in by hand to avoid cross-threading, and tighten it firmly with the socket. It needs to seal, but does not need to be cranked as hard as possible. Replace any cap or insulation you removed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Close the drain valve, open the cold supply, and let the tank fill with the hot faucet still open. When the faucet runs steadily with no air sputtering, the tank is full. Check the anode fitting for leaks, then restore power or turn the gas control back on. If you see a slow drip at the fitting, tighten slightly or redo the thread seal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When Anode Replacement Is Worth It
          </h2>

          <p>
            The case for replacing an anode is strongest on a heater that is in otherwise good condition and has years of expected life left. A new anode on a five-year-old tank that has never leaked costs little and restores protection before corrosion has done lasting damage. That is one of the best-value maintenance jobs in the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case is weaker on an old heater. If a tank is twelve years old and the anode has been gone for years, the steel has likely been corroding without protection, and a new anode will not reverse that damage. At that point, the money is usually better spent toward a replacement. Our <a href="/water-heater-replacement-cost/">water heater replacement cost guide</a> covers what that costs, and our <a href="/how-long-do-water-heaters-last/">guide to how long water heaters last</a> explains typical lifespans.
          </p>

          <p>
            Another factor is access. If the anode is seized, the heater is in a tight space, or the head is rounded, the cost of a plumber may tip the balance, especially on an older unit. For a younger heater with easy access, it is a job most homeowners can do themselves in an hour.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are buying a new heater, it is worth noting which anode it ships with and how easy it is to reach. Heaters with longer warranties often have better anode protection. Our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">best gas water heater guide</a> and <a href="/water-heater-brands/">water heater brands comparison</a> are good places to compare models.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, make anode checks part of a routine. Combined with an annual flush and a quick look at the pressure relief valve and connections, they give you a clear picture of the heater's health and help you plan its replacement rather than discovering the need through a leak.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Waiting until the tank leaks to check the anode</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                By the time a tank leaks, the anode has usually been gone for a long time and the steel has corroded through. Anode replacement is preventive, not a repair. Check it at three to five years, sooner with softened water, and replace it while the tank is still sound.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Removing the anode to stop a smell</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Taking the anode out may stop the rotten-egg odor, but it leaves the tank unprotected and can void the warranty. The usual fixes are an aluminum-zinc or powered anode, sometimes combined with disinfecting the tank according to the manufacturer's instructions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Twisting the whole tank with the breaker bar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Seized anodes can take a lot of force, and without bracing, the tank can rotate and stress or break the water, gas or vent connections. Have a helper hold the tank, use a strap, or try an impact wrench. Stop and reassess if the tank starts to move.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the power on during the job</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                You will be draining water from the tank, and on an electric heater an element that switches on without water around it can burn out in seconds. Turn off the breaker or gas control before draining, and leave it off until the tank is completely refilled and the air is purged.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a rigid rod with no room to install it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A standard rod needs roughly the height of the tank in clearance above it. Measure before buying. If the heater is close to the ceiling or under shelving, buy a flexible segmented rod in the material you want instead of discovering the problem halfway through the job.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a water heater anode rod be replaced?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Check the anode at around three to five years and then every year or two afterward, sooner if you have a water softener. Replace it when six inches or more of the core wire is exposed or the rod is badly thinned. How long an anode lasts depends heavily on water chemistry, so there is no fixed replacement interval that suits every home.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to replace an anode rod?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Standard magnesium or aluminum rods typically cost well under fifty dollars at retail, with flexible rods costing a little more and powered anodes costing several times as much. Doing it yourself costs only the part and an hour. A plumber will usually charge a service call plus labor, which is more if the rod is seized or hard to reach.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size socket do I need for a water heater anode rod?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most residential anode rods use a 1-1/16 inch hex head, so a 1-1/16 inch socket is the standard tool. A six-point impact socket grips best. Use a long breaker bar for leverage, since anodes are often tight or seized, and brace the tank so it does not twist while you turn the rod.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is magnesium or aluminum better for an anode rod?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Magnesium gives stronger protection and suits soft to moderate water, but wears faster and is more associated with rotten-egg smells. Aluminum-zinc lasts longer, often suits hard water, and helps reduce odors. If you have a persistent smell or want to avoid replacements, a powered anode is another option. Check that your choice is approved for your heater.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a bad anode rod cause a rotten-egg smell?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The anode itself does not create the smell, but a magnesium anode can feed the reaction that does. Sulfate-reducing bacteria in the water use hydrogen from the anode reaction to produce hydrogen sulfide. Switching to an aluminum-zinc or powered anode is the common fix. If cold water smells as well, the source is the water supply rather than the heater.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do tankless water heaters have anode rods?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most tankless water heaters do not have anode rods, because they do not store water in a steel tank the way tank heaters do. Their main maintenance task is descaling the heat exchanger. Some tank-style heat pump water heaters do have anodes, though, so check the manual for any heater that stores water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I replace an anode rod if I have low ceiling clearance?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A flexible or segmented anode is made of short linked sections that bend as you feed it into the tank, so it fits where a rigid rod will not. You may need to bend or cut the old rod to get it out. Flexible rods come in magnesium and aluminum-zinc versions, so you can still choose the type you need.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

