        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The anode rod is the cheapest part of a tank water heater and one of the most important. Check it at three to five years, replace it when the core wire shows, and choose the material based on your water: magnesium for most homes, aluminum-zinc or powered for hard water or a sulfur smell.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            On a heater that is still sound, a new anode is an hour of work that can buy years of extra life. On one that is already old and corroded, it is usually better to put that money toward a replacement, and our gas water heater picks are the place to start.
          </p>
        </div>

